itle, role, or sections), I can adjust. <|COMPLETE|>", "CHURCH:A religious institution cited as a viewpoint superior to the landscape, influencing perception and partitioning of landscape", "BALZAC:BALZAC is a French novelist referenced in the text as shaping the problem of unity in art (through the idea that unity results from the work of Balzac’s novels and Foreword), and as having a unique contribution to the concept of style", "EMILE BREHIER:Scholar who provides a reconstruction of Stoic thought and is cited in the passage as asserting that incorporeal events are not causes but quasi-causes, illustrated by the example of the scalpel cutting through flesh.)<|COMPLETE|>(\"entity\"", "STOICS:", "ALICE:ALICE is a fictional character from Lewis Carroll's works who functions as a comprehensive analytic device across a range of philosophical and literary discussions. In Carroll's exploration of serialization, Alice experiences shelf-related imagery and serves as an example of how events are serialized. In analyses of sense, naming, and morality, she participates in interactions with the Duchess, illustrating social dynamics and ethical considerations within the narrative. She is known for shrinking and growing and for encounters with spheres, emblematic of bodily and conceptual transformations. Alice is invoked to illuminate the interplay between sense and denotation and to clarify the dynamics of serial forms. She is described as the case of a double adventure—the becoming mad and the lost name—used to demonstrate paradoxes that arise when identity, memory, and linguistic reference collide. In Humpty Dumpty's discourse, Alice's singularities are assimilated into the ordinary arrangement of an organ (eye, nose, mouth), placing her among the commonplace of an all-too-regular face; Humpty Dumpty will not recognize her. Across these uses, Alice remains central to discussions of surface versus depth and incorporeal doubles; she undergoes transformations as she navigates borders between physical bodies and the incorporeal, thereby shaping a coherent picture of how language, perception, and identity interact within Carroll's imagined worlds."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_756e7c29dfd0d903e6e7e869320d78e747cba30b12d9c7e7348dc35fae44164f_v2"}